  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes two lysosome-centered routes to cell death. On the left, lysosomotropic compounds, reactive oxygen species (ROS), BAX, and caspase-8/caspase-10 all converge on the lysosomal membrane, promoting membrane permeabilization. Anti-apoptotic proteins such as BCL-2, BCL-xL, and MCL-1 are shown as inhibitors of this step, indicating that they can restrain lysosome-driven death signaling. Once the lysosomal membrane is compromised, the pathway leads toward cell death.\n\nA second route begins with buildup of lysosomal contents, labeled as lipofuscin or the lipids glucosylceramide and glucosylsphingosine. This accumulation causes lysosomal congestion, which is linked to activation of necroptosis-related markers pRIPK3 and pMLKL in a swollen, irregular cell. That state is followed by release of lysosomal cathepsins and other hydrolases, again ending in cell death.\n\nOverall, the image emphasizes that lysosomes are not just degradative organelles but active regulators of lethal stress responses. Cell death can result either from direct lysosomal membrane damage triggered by stress signals or from pathological storage and congestion that culminate in enzyme release and necroptotic signaling.",
